# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Class 4 Maths Chapter 3: Patterns Around Us - 20 Slides Data"""

slides = [{'id': 1, 'title': 'Coconut Tree Grids', 'section': 'Let Us Count', 'speech': 'Welcome to our mathematics laboratory. Today we explore fascinating patterns in counting, grouping, and number properties. Observe this orchard where a farmer has planted coconut trees in neat, uniform rows and columns. When items are arranged in a rectangular array, we do not need to count each tree one by one. Instead, we can observe the structure. Notice that there are four horizontal rows and six vertical columns of coconut trees. To determine the total number of trees, we multiply the number of rows by the number of columns. Multiplying four by six yields twenty-four trees in total. This grid structure allows quick mental calculations. Now consider plucking coconuts from these trees. Suppose five ripe coconuts are harvested from every single tree in this orchard. To find the overall harvest, we multiply the twenty-four trees by five coconuts. Breaking twenty-four into twenty and four makes mental multiplication simple: twenty times five equals one hundred, and four times five equals twenty. Combining them gives one hundred twenty coconuts harvested in total. Grid arrangements turn repetitive addition into fast multiplication. Try adjusting the rows and columns in the interactive orchard to observe how array patterns transform counting tasks.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'An orchard has 5 rows with 7 coconut trees in each row. How many trees are there in total?', 'options': ['30 trees', '35 trees', '40 trees', '42 trees'], 'ans': 1, 'exp': 'Using array multiplication, 5 rows × 7 trees per row = 35 trees in total.'}, {'q': 'If 4 coconuts are harvested from each of the 35 trees, what is the total coconut harvest?', 'options': ['120 coconuts', '140 coconuts', '150 coconuts', '160 coconuts'], 'ans': 1, 'exp': 'Multiplying 35 trees × 4 coconuts gives: (30 × 4) + (5 × 4) = 120 + 20 = 140 coconuts.'}]}, {'id': 2, 'title': 'Stacked Cups Columns', 'section': 'Let Us Count', 'speech': 'Let us now visit a traditional pottery workshop. Here, a skilled potter has organized freshly baked clay cups and plates into orderly vertical stacks. Stacking items vertically in equal columns creates an excellent visual pattern for multiplication. Observe the workbench layout. There are five separate stacks of clay tea cups standing side by side. Each vertical stack contains exactly six cups nested securely. Instead of counting every cup individually from left to right, we identify the repeated group size. Here, we have five groups of six cups. Multiplying five by six gives thirty clay cups in total. Beside the cups, we see stacks of circular dining plates. There are four vertical stacks, and each stack contains eight plates. Multiplying four stacks by eight plates reveals that thirty-two plates are organized on the pottery bench. Grouping identical stacks into columns and rows is a powerful spatial habit used across many daily professions. It eliminates counting errors, speeds up inventory audits, and prevents accidental breakage on busy production floors. Whether stacking ceramic bowls, storage boxes, or metal containers, column multiplication remains the fastest counting strategy. Use the interactive stack builder below to vary the number of columns and items per stack, observing how total counts scale dynamically.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'A potter arranges 6 vertical stacks with 7 clay cups in each stack. What is the total count of cups?', 'options': ['36 cups', '42 cups', '48 cups', '49 cups'], 'ans': 1, 'exp': 'Multiply 6 stacks × 7 cups per stack = 42 cups in total.'}, {'q': 'If 3 cups are removed from each of the 6 stacks, how many cups remain on the workbench?', 'options': ['20 cups', '24 cups', '28 cups', '30 cups'], 'ans': 1, 'exp': 'Each stack now has 7 - 3 = 4 cups. Total remaining cups = 6 × 4 = 24 cups.'}]}, {'id': 3, 'title': 'Layered Sweet Trays', 'section': 'Let Us Count', 'speech': 'In sweet shops across India, festive confectioners arrange mouth-watering coconut laddoos and milk pedas in beautiful symmetrical patterns. Examine this square display tray closely. The confectioner has arranged the sweets in a distinctive cross-shaped geometric layout. Coconut laddoos form the central cross arms, with five laddoos placed in a central horizontal row and four more completing the vertical arm. This makes nine coconut laddoos per tray. In the four outer corner quadrants, delicate milk pedas are arranged in clusters of four. Four corner clusters of four milk pedas each equal sixteen pedas on each single tray. When trays are stacked in multiple identical tiers, the total quantity of each confection scales systematically. For example, if three identical trays are prepared for a wedding celebration, we multiply the contents of one tray by three. Three trays will hold three times nine, which equals twenty-seven coconut laddoos, and three times sixteen, which equals forty-eight milk pedas. Symmetrical geometric packaging ensures equal portioning, neat artistic presentation, and rapid visual accounting for large festival orders. Combining shape symmetry with multiplication makes complex catering inventories effortless. Explore the multi-tiered sweet tray simulator below to inspect individual trays or calculate combined batch totals for large festive gatherings.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'A sweet tray contains 9 coconut laddoos and 16 milk pedas. How many total sweets are on one tray?', 'options': ['23 sweets', '25 sweets', '27 sweets', '30 sweets'], 'ans': 1, 'exp': 'Total sweets on one tray = 9 laddoos + 16 pedas = 25 sweets.'}, {'q': 'How many total milk pedas are packed across 4 identical sweet trays?', 'options': ['48 pedas', '56 pedas', '64 pedas', '72 pedas'], 'ans': 2, 'exp': 'Each tray holds 16 pedas. For 4 trays: 4 × 16 = 64 milk pedas in total.'}]}, {'id': 4, 'title': 'Banknote Coin Patterns', 'section': 'Patterns with Money', 'speech': 'Money systems provide fascinating patterns combining numerical values with geometric symmetry. Young learners often practice organizing coins and banknotes into radial mandalas and balanced arrays. Observe the pattern on our virtual cash table. At the center sits a neat stack of four ten-rupee currency notes. Around this central core, coins are placed symmetrically: four five-rupee coins in the cardinal compass directions, eight two-rupee coins at the intermediate diagonals, and an outer circular ring of eight one-rupee coins. To calculate the total monetary value, we group by denomination carefully. Four ten-rupee notes equal forty rupees. Four five-rupee coins equal twenty rupees. Eight two-rupee coins equal sixteen rupees. Finally, eight one-rupee coins equal eight rupees. Adding these partial sums step by step reveals the full amount: forty plus twenty is sixty, plus sixteen is seventy-six, plus eight gives eighty-four rupees. Notice that grouping identical denominations makes money calculation both orderly, reliable, and completely foolproof. Symmetrical money arrangements also demonstrate clearly that physical coin count does not directly match monetary value, because higher-denomination coins carry greater buying power. Step forward and use the interactive money board below to arrange coins into symmetrical geometric rosettes and verify denomination totals instantly.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'A money pattern contains 5 ten-rupee notes, 4 five-rupee coins, and 6 two-rupee coins. What is the total value?', 'options': ['₹76', '₹82', '₹86', '₹92'], 'ans': 1, 'exp': 'Value = (5 × 10) + (4 × 5) + (6 × 2) = 50 + 20 + 12 = ₹82.'}, {'q': 'Which coin collection has a greater monetary value: 7 five-rupee coins or 16 two-rupee coins?', 'options': ['7 five-rupee coins', '16 two-rupee coins', 'Both are equal', 'Cannot be determined'], 'ans': 0, 'exp': '7 × ₹5 = ₹35, while 16 × ₹2 = ₹32. Therefore, 7 five-rupee coins represent a greater monetary value.'}]}, {'id': 5, 'title': 'Building Target Amounts', 'section': 'Patterns with Money', 'speech': 'In everyday transactions, we frequently combine different currency denominations to pay exact prices without needing change. Suppose we must assemble a target amount of thirty-six rupees. We can use three ten-rupee notes to make thirty rupees, one five-rupee coin to reach thirty-five, and one single one-rupee coin to reach thirty-six rupees. Notice how we start with the largest practical denomination to minimize the total number of physical tokens needed. Now consider a larger target of one hundred twenty-five rupees. We can select one hundred-rupee note, one twenty-rupee note, and one five-rupee coin, using just three currency items. Alternatively, if no hundred-rupee note is available, we could use twelve ten-rupee notes and one five-rupee coin. For one hundred eighty-three rupees, we can combine one hundred-rupee note, one fifty-rupee note, three ten-rupee notes, one two-rupee coin, and one one-rupee coin. Understanding multiple combinations of notes and coins develops strong number sense and flexible place-value thinking. Large denominations represent bundles of tens and hundreds, directly mirroring our decimal place-value system. Mastering currency combinations ensures confidence in local markets, banks, and grocery billing. Experiment on the interactive currency builder to assemble target monetary amounts using the fewest possible coins and notes.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'What is the minimum number of notes and coins needed to form ₹87 using standard Indian denominations (₹50, ₹20, ₹10, ₹5, ₹2, ₹1)?', 'options': ['4 items', '5 items', '6 items', '7 items'], 'ans': 1, 'exp': 'Optimal combination: 1 × ₹50 + 1 × ₹20 + 1 × ₹10 + 1 × ₹5 + 1 × ₹2 = ₹87 (5 items).'}, {'q': 'Can ₹36 be formed using only ₹5 coins and ₹2 coins?', 'options': ['No, impossible', 'Yes, with 6 five-rupee coins and 3 two-rupee coins', 'Yes, with 7 five-rupee coins and 1 two-rupee coin', 'Yes, with 4 five-rupee coins and 7 two-rupee coins'], 'ans': 1, 'exp': '6 × ₹5 = ₹30, and 3 × ₹2 = ₹6. 30 + 6 = ₹36.'}]}, {'id': 6, 'title': 'Visualizing Quantity Distributions', 'section': 'Two Ways', 'speech': 'A given quantity of objects can often be arranged in more than one distinct geometric pattern. Examining these alternative arrangements helps us uncover hidden mathematical properties such as symmetry, divisibility, and pairing. Consider twelve shiny brass coins placed on a mat. One young explorer arranges these twelve coins into two neat horizontal rows of six coins each. In this layout, every coin in the upper row has an exact partner directly beneath it in the lower row. It looks like a balanced parade of two-by-two marchers. Another young explorer takes the very same twelve coins and arranges them into three columns of four coins each, forming a compact rectangular block. Both arrangements represent the exact same quantity of twelve, yet each highlights a different mathematical relationship. The two-row arrangement demonstrates that twelve splits evenly into two equal halves of six, with no leftover coins. The three-column arrangement demonstrates that twelve is a multiple of three and four. Whenever we distribute quantities into geometric patterns, we visually prove arithmetic truths and foster spatial reasoning. Observing numbers in multiple shapes strengthens our conceptual grasp of factors and fair sharing. Use the interactive distribution canvas below to drag coins into pairs, triangular pyramids, or rectangular arrays, comparing two distinct perspectives side by side.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'Which of the following geometric rectangular arrays CANNOT be formed using exactly 14 coins without leftovers?', 'options': ['2 rows of 7 coins', '7 rows of 2 coins', '3 rows of 4 coins', '1 row of 14 coins'], 'ans': 2, 'exp': '3 rows of 4 coins equals 12 coins, leaving 2 coins left over. 14 is not divisible by 3.'}, {'q': 'If 16 coins are arranged into 2 equal rows, how many coins are in each row?', 'options': ['6 coins', '7 coins', '8 coins', '9 coins'], 'ans': 2, 'exp': 'Dividing 16 coins evenly into 2 rows yields 16 ÷ 2 = 8 coins per row.'}]}, {'id': 7, 'title': 'Concept of Pairs', 'section': 'Two Ways', 'speech': 'In nature, daily life, and mathematics, the concept of a pair is fundamental. A pair always consists of exactly two matching items working together. Think of familiar examples from your home and classroom: a pair of shoes, a pair of socks, a pair of gloves, or a pair of eyes. When children line up for school assembly in pairs, they walk side by side in twos. This pairing process gives us a powerful method for testing any collection of items. Imagine placing a collection of buttons on your desk. To test the collection, you begin grouping the buttons two by two. You take two buttons and push them together into a pair. You take another two buttons and form a second pair. You continue this two-by-two grouping repeatedly until all buttons have been examined. Two possible outcomes can occur. Either every single button finds a partner and forms a complete pair, or exactly one solitary button is left over without a partner. This universal test divides all whole numbers into two great families: even numbers and odd numbers. Step into the pairing laboratory below, tap the group button, and watch items automatically couple into pairs to discover the remaining balance.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'How many items are there in 6 complete pairs of socks?', 'options': ['8 socks', '10 socks', '12 socks', '14 socks'], 'ans': 2, 'exp': 'Each pair has 2 socks. Therefore, 6 pairs × 2 socks per pair = 12 socks.'}, {'q': 'If you have 11 pencils and group them into pairs, how many pencils will be left without a partner?', 'options': ['0 pencils', '1 pencil', '2 pencils', '3 pencils'], 'ans': 1, 'exp': '11 pencils form 5 complete pairs (10 pencils) with exactly 1 pencil left unpaired.'}]}, {'id': 8, 'title': 'Even Number Pairing', 'section': 'Two Ways', 'speech': 'Now let us formalize the mathematical definition of an even number. Any whole quantity that can be divided completely into pairs of two without leaving any leftover item is called an even number. Consider the number eight. If we take eight marbles and pair them up, we get four complete pairs. Not a single marble is left alone. Therefore, eight is an even number. Now look at the number ten. Ten marbles form five complete pairs with zero remainder. Thus, ten is an even number. Notice an important visual feature: when an even number of dots is arranged in two parallel rows, both rows have the exact same length. The structure is completely symmetrical, like a solid two-lane road. Because every item has a partner, even numbers can always be split into two equal whole halves. Half of eight is four, and half of ten is five. Zero is also classified as an even number, because zero items leave zero remainder when grouped by two. Even numbers represent harmony, balance, and complete pairing in arithmetic. Use the interactive parity tester below to test numbers like six, twelve, and eighteen, observing how every dot locks securely into a matching partner.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'Which of the following numbers can be divided into pairs of 2 with zero remainder?', 'options': ['7', '11', '14', '17'], 'ans': 2, 'exp': '14 can be divided into 7 complete pairs (14 ÷ 2 = 7) with 0 remainder, making 14 an even number.'}, {'q': 'If an even number of children are arranged into pairs, what can be said about the remaining unpaired children?', 'options': ['There is always 1 child left', 'There are always 2 children left', 'There are exactly 0 children left', 'It varies depending on the number'], 'ans': 2, 'exp': 'By definition, an even number leaves exactly 0 unpaired items when grouped in twos.'}]}, {'id': 9, 'title': 'Odd Number Remainder', 'section': 'Two Ways', 'speech': 'Having explored even numbers, let us examine the complementary family: odd numbers. Any whole quantity that leaves exactly one unpaired item when divided into groups of two is called an odd number. Consider seven ripe mangoes. If we pair them up, we get three complete pairs of two mangoes each, but one solitary mango remains without a partner. Because of this leftover item, seven is an odd number. Now consider nine pebbles. Nine pebbles form four complete pairs, with one extra pebble left on the side. Therefore, nine is an odd number. Notice the characteristic visual shape of an odd number when arranged in two parallel rows. One row will always be exactly one unit longer than the other row. This creates a distinct protruding step or ledge at the end of the line. Because of this extra unit, odd numbers can never be split into two equal whole numbers. If you try to share an odd number equally between two friends without cutting, one friend will always receive one more item, or one item must be set aside. Odd numbers possess dynamic energy and an unmistakable signature remainder of one. Use the interactive odd pairing tool below to test numbers like five, nine, and fifteen, observing the lonely remainder dot.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'What is the remainder when an odd number is divided by 2?', 'options': ['Always 0', 'Always 1', 'Always 2', 'Depends on the number'], 'ans': 1, 'exp': 'By definition, dividing any odd number by 2 leaves a remainder of exactly 1.'}, {'q': 'If 13 marbles are paired up, how many complete pairs and leftover marbles are obtained?', 'options': ['5 pairs and 3 leftovers', '6 pairs and 0 leftovers', '6 pairs and 1 leftover', '7 pairs and 1 leftover'], 'ans': 2, 'exp': '13 ÷ 2 = 6 complete pairs (12 marbles) with exactly 1 leftover marble.'}]}, {'id': 10, 'title': 'Numbers One Twenty', 'section': 'Two Ways', 'speech': 'Let us systematically investigate the numbers from one to twenty to observe how even and odd quantities behave in sequence. Beginning with one, a single item cannot form a pair, so one is odd. Adding another gives two, which forms one complete pair, so two is even. Three leaves one remainder, making it odd. Four forms two complete pairs, making it even. Continuing this check reveals a remarkable pattern: one, three, five, seven, nine, eleven, thirteen, fifteen, seventeen, and nineteen are odd. Meanwhile, two, four, six, eight, ten, twelve, fourteen, sixteen, eighteen, and twenty are even. Notice that every number in the two-times multiplication table is an even number. When we recite two, four, six, eight, ten, twelve, fourteen, sixteen, eighteen, and twenty, we are counting exclusively by even numbers. This occurs because every multiple of two is formed by taking groups of two, ensuring complete pairing by its very definition. Even and odd numbers alternate in a strict, unbroken rhythmic cycle: odd, even, odd, even, repeating endlessly across the number line. Take a moment to test this pairing progression in the interactive number classifier below to see dots pair up dynamically from one to twenty.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'Are all numbers in the 2-times multiplication table even numbers?', 'options': ['No, some are odd', 'Yes, every multiple of 2 is an even number', 'Only numbers greater than 10', 'Only numbers ending in 0'], 'ans': 1, 'exp': 'Yes, every multiple of 2 is formed by complete pairs of 2, so every product in the 2-times table is even.'}, {'q': 'Between 1 and 20 inclusive, how many numbers are odd and how many are even?', 'options': ['8 odd and 12 even', '9 odd and 11 even', '10 odd and 10 even', '11 odd and 9 even'], 'ans': 2, 'exp': 'From 1 to 20, there are exactly 10 odd numbers and 10 even numbers, perfectly balanced.'}]}, {'id': 11, 'title': 'Crayon Number Grid', 'section': 'Crayons Arrangement', 'speech': 'Visual charts help us spot numerical patterns effortlessly. Imagine a large classroom chart displaying numbers from one to fifty, arranged like orderly boxes of colorful drawing crayons in rows of ten. Let us practice a visual classification exercise on this grid. We place a circle around every odd number and draw a square box around every even number. As you complete the chart, observe the columns. All the numbers in the first column end in one: one, eleven, twenty-one, thirty-one, forty-one. Every single one is circled because they are all odd. The second column contains numbers ending in two: two, twelve, twenty-two, thirty-two, forty-two. Every number in this column is boxed with a square because they are all even. Moving across the grid reveals that columns one, three, five, seven, and nine consist entirely of circled odd numbers. Conversely, columns two, four, six, eight, and ten consist entirely of boxed even numbers. The grid splits into alternating vertical stripes of odd and even numbers. This visual striping confirms that the ending digit alone dictates whether any number is odd or even. Step into the interactive crayon grid below and toggle markers to highlight vertical parity stripes from one to fifty.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'In a 1–50 number grid with rows of 10, which column contains only even numbers?', 'options': ['Column ending in 3', 'Column ending in 5', 'Column ending in 6', 'Column ending in 7'], 'ans': 2, 'exp': 'The column ending in 6 (6, 16, 26, 36, 46) contains only even numbers.'}, {'q': 'What shape marker would you place on number 37 in the crayon grid?', 'options': ['A square box (even)', 'A circle (odd)', 'A triangle', 'No marker'], 'ans': 1, 'exp': '37 ends in 7, which is an odd digit, so 37 is odd and receives a circle marker.'}]}, {'id': 12, 'title': 'Book Page Parity', 'section': 'Crayons Arrangement', 'speech': 'A wonderful real-world example of alternating number parity can be observed simply by opening any printed textbook or storybook. Pick up a mathematics book and open it flat on your reading desk. Look at the two facing pages in front of you. In standard book publishing, the left-hand page always bears an even page number, such as twenty-four, forty-six, or eighty-eight. Meanwhile, the right-hand page directly opposite always displays an odd page number, such as twenty-five, forty-seven, or eighty-nine. Why does this invariant structure occur? Because a book begins with page one on the right-hand front side of the opening leaf. Flipping each printed sheet advances the reading count by two pages: an even back page on the left and a new odd page on the right. Therefore, every two-page spread consists of an even page followed immediately by an odd page. The sum of the two facing page numbers is always an odd number, because an even number plus an odd number always yields an odd total. For example, page fourteen plus page fifteen equals twenty-nine. Use the interactive virtual book below to flip through spreads, inspecting page numbers and verifying their alternating parity.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'If the left page of an open book is page 42, what is the number of the facing right page?', 'options': ['Page 41', 'Page 43', 'Page 44', 'Page 40'], 'ans': 1, 'exp': 'The facing right page directly follows the left page, so it is 42 + 1 = 43, which is an odd number.'}, {'q': 'What is the parity of the sum of two facing page numbers (an even page plus an odd page)?', 'options': ['Always even', 'Always odd', 'Sometimes even, sometimes odd', 'Always a multiple of 10'], 'ans': 1, 'exp': 'An even number plus an odd number always equals an odd number (e.g., 20 + 21 = 41).'}]}, {'id': 13, 'title': 'Multi-Digit Parity Rule', 'section': 'Even and Odd Number Exploration & Properties', 'speech': 'How do we determine if very large numbers, like three hundred, four hundred fifteen, or seven hundred eighty-eight, are even or odd? Do we need to divide the entire large number by two, or draw hundreds of pairing dots? The delightful answer is no! We only need to look at a single digit: the digit in the ones place. Every bundle of ten, hundred, or thousand is formed by multiplying by ten. Since ten is an even number, ten, twenty, one hundred, and one thousand are always completely divisible into pairs of two. Therefore, all the tens, hundreds, and thousands digits pair up with zero remainder. Parity depends entirely on the leftover ones digit. Here is the universal rule: if a number ends in zero, two, four, six, or eight, the entire number is even. If a number ends in one, three, five, seven, or nine, the entire number is odd. Look at forty-six: it ends in six, so it is even. Look at sixty-seven: it ends in seven, so it is odd. Three hundred ends in zero, making it even. Four hundred fifteen ends in five, making it odd. Try testing massive multi-digit numbers in the interactive parity scanner below to witness this fast rule in action.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'Which of the following numbers is an odd number: 154, 300, 415, 78?', 'options': ['154', '300', '415', '78'], 'ans': 2, 'exp': '415 ends in 5, which is an odd digit, making 415 an odd number.'}, {'q': 'A 4-digit number ends in 8. Is the number even or odd?', 'options': ['Odd', 'Even', 'Cannot tell without knowing all digits', 'Both even and odd'], 'ans': 1, 'exp': 'Any number ending in 8 is an even number, regardless of how many digits precede it.'}]}, {'id': 14, 'title': 'Digit Permutation Parity', 'section': 'Even and Odd Number Exploration & Properties', 'speech': 'Let us explore how rearranging digits alters the parity of formed numbers. Suppose you are given two number cards: the digit one and the digit six. What two-digit numbers can you assemble using both digits without repeating? You can form sixteen, or you can reverse the digits to form sixty-one. Now examine their parity. In the number sixteen, six is placed in the ones position. Because six is an even digit, sixteen is an even number. However, when we reverse the digits to make sixty-one, the digit one occupies the ones position. Because one is an odd digit, sixty-one is an odd number. Notice that changing the position of digits completely flips the parity. The tens digit has zero influence on whether the number is even or odd; only the final digit in the ones place matters. Now consider the digits three and five. Reversing them produces thirty-five and fifty-three. Since both three and five are odd digits, both assembled numbers are odd. If you choose two even digits, like four and eight, both forty-eight and eighty-four remain even. Experiment on the interactive digit permutation table below to drag digits into place and observe how position alters parity.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'Using the digits 2 and 7, what is the even number that can be formed?', 'options': ['72', '27', 'Both 72 and 27', 'Neither is even'], 'ans': 0, 'exp': '72 ends in 2 (an even digit), so 72 is even. 27 ends in 7 (an odd digit), so 27 is odd.'}, {'q': 'If you are given the digits 4 and 8, how many odd 2-digit numbers can you form using both digits?', 'options': ['0 numbers', '1 number', '2 numbers', '4 numbers'], 'ans': 0, 'exp': 'Both digits (4 and 8) are even, so both possible numbers (48 and 84) are even. 0 odd numbers can be formed.'}]}, {'id': 15, 'title': 'Consecutive Number Alternation', 'section': 'Even and Odd Number Exploration & Properties', 'speech': 'Whenever we count in order along the number line, numbers alternate between even and odd with perfect mathematical regularity. Let us observe any ten consecutive numbers, such as forty-one through fifty. Forty-one is odd, forty-two is even, forty-three is odd, forty-four is even, forty-five is odd, forty-six is even, forty-seven is odd, forty-eight is even, forty-nine is odd, and fifty is even. Why does this strict alternating pattern never fail? Because adding one to any number changes its pairing balance. If you start with an even number where all items are paired, adding one extra item creates an unpaired remainder, producing an odd number. Conversely, if you start with an odd number containing an unpaired remainder, adding one extra item completes the pair, producing an even number. This principle leads to a fundamental neighbor rule: the numbers immediately preceding and directly following any odd number are always even. Similarly, the numbers immediately preceding and following any even number are always odd. Every odd number is cushioned between two even neighbors, and every even number is cushioned between two odd neighbors. Explore the interactive consecutive number tracker below to slide across the number line and observe this rhythmic neighbor heartbeat.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'What can be said about the two numbers directly neighboring an odd number on the number line?', 'options': ['Both neighbors are odd', 'Both neighbors are even', 'One neighbor is odd, one is even', 'It depends on the number'], 'ans': 1, 'exp': 'The predecessor (number - 1) and successor (number + 1) of any odd number are both even numbers.'}, {'q': 'In any sequence of 5 consecutive whole numbers, what is the minimum number of even numbers?', 'options': ['1', '2', '3', '4'], 'ans': 1, 'exp': 'A sequence of 5 numbers can be either (Odd, Even, Odd, Even, Odd) with 2 even numbers, or (Even, Odd, Even, Odd, Even) with 3 even numbers. The minimum is 2.'}]}, {'id': 16, 'title': 'Range Parity Balance', 'section': 'Even and Odd Number Exploration & Properties', 'speech': 'To conclude our investigation of number patterns, let us examine parity balance across broad numerical intervals. Consider all the counting numbers from one to one hundred. How many of these one hundred numbers are even, and how many are odd? Because whole numbers alternate strictly between odd and even in continuous pairs, every two consecutive numbers contain exactly one odd number and one even number. In the first ten numbers, there are five odd numbers and five even numbers. In the next ten numbers, from eleven to twenty, there are again five odd and five even numbers. Since one hundred consists of ten equal decades of ten numbers each, we multiply ten decades by five. This reveals that between one and one hundred, there are exactly fifty odd numbers and fifty even numbers. The two families divide the entire century of numbers into two perfectly equal halves. Furthermore, the sum of any two even numbers is always even, and the sum of any two odd numbers is also always even, because their two solitary remainders pair together to make another complete pair. Use the interactive range balance simulator below to inspect intervals and visualize the harmonious fifty-fifty split.', 'checkpoints': [{'q': 'Between 1 and 100 inclusive, how many odd numbers are there?', 'options': ['45', '49', '50', '51'], 'ans': 2, 'exp': 'Exactly half of the first 100 counting numbers are odd (50 odd numbers) and half are even (50 even numbers).'}, {'q': 'What is the parity of the sum of two odd numbers (for example, 7 + 5 = 12)?', 'options': ['Always odd', 'Always even', 'Sometimes odd, sometimes even', 'Zero'], 'ans': 1, 'exp': 'Odd + Odd is always Even, because the two leftover remainders of 1 combine to form a complete pair of 2.'}]}, {'id': 17, 'title': 'Challenge 1', 'section': 'Mastery Challenges', 'speech': '', 'quiz': [{'id': 1, 'q': 'An orchard has 6 rows of coconut trees with 8 trees in each row. How many coconut trees are planted in total?', 'options': ['42 trees', '46 trees', '48 trees', '54 trees'], 'ans': 2, 'hint': 'Multiply the number of rows by the number of trees in each column.', 'explanation': '6 rows × 8 trees per row = 48 coconut trees in total.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Identify the grid dimensions: 6 horizontal rows and 8 vertical columns.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Apply rectangular array multiplication: Total = Rows × Columns.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Calculate: 6 × 8 = 48 trees.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 2, 'q': 'If 5 coconuts are plucked from each of the 48 trees, what is the total number of coconuts harvested?', 'options': ['200 coconuts', '220 coconuts', '240 coconuts', '250 coconuts'], 'ans': 2, 'hint': 'Multiply 48 by 5 by splitting 48 into 40 + 8.', 'explanation': '(40 × 5) + (8 × 5) = 200 + 40 = 240 coconuts.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Total trees = 48. Plucked coconuts per tree = 5.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Multiply 48 × 5.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Break down: 40 × 5 = 200, and 8 × 5 = 40.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Sum: 200 + 40 = 240 coconuts.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 3, 'q': 'A potter arranges 7 vertical stacks of clay cups. Each stack has 6 cups. How many cups are on the bench?', 'options': ['36 cups', '42 cups', '48 cups', '49 cups'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'Calculate 7 groups of 6 items.', 'explanation': '7 stacks × 6 cups per stack = 42 cups in total.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Identify the number of stacks: 7 vertical columns.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Count items per column: 6 cups.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Calculate: 7 × 6 = 42 cups.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 4, 'q': 'On another table, there are 5 stacks of plates with 8 plates in each stack. If 6 plates are sold, how many plates remain?', 'options': ['32 plates', '34 plates', '36 plates', '40 plates'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'Find the total number of plates first, then subtract the 6 sold plates.', 'explanation': 'Total plates = 5 × 8 = 40. Remaining = 40 - 6 = 34 plates.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Calculate initial plates: 5 stacks × 8 plates = 40 plates.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Subtract the 6 sold plates: 40 - 6 = 34 plates remaining.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 5, 'q': 'A sweet tray has 9 coconut laddoos and 16 milk pedas. How many total sweets are arranged in 3 such identical trays?', 'options': ['65 sweets', '70 sweets', '75 sweets', '80 sweets'], 'ans': 2, 'hint': 'Find the total sweets in one tray, then multiply by 3.', 'explanation': 'One tray = 9 + 16 = 25 sweets. 3 trays = 3 × 25 = 75 sweets.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Count sweets per tray: 9 laddoos + 16 pedas = 25 sweets.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Multiply by 3 trays: 3 × 25 = 75 total sweets.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 6, 'q': 'How many more milk pedas than coconut laddoos are there across 4 identical sweet trays?', 'options': ['24 more pedas', '28 more pedas', '32 more pedas', '36 more pedas'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'In one tray, pedas exceed laddoos by 16 - 9 = 7 sweets.', 'explanation': 'Difference per tray = 16 - 9 = 7 pedas. Across 4 trays: 4 × 7 = 28 more pedas.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Difference in one tray = 16 pedas - 9 laddoos = 7 pedas.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Scale for 4 trays: 4 × 7 = 28 more pedas.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 7, 'q': 'A money pattern has 6 ten-rupee notes, 4 five-rupee coins, and 8 two-rupee coins. What is the total monetary value?', 'options': ['₹88', '₹92', '₹96', '₹100'], 'ans': 2, 'hint': 'Group and multiply: 6 × 10, 4 × 5, and 8 × 2, then add all values.', 'explanation': '(6 × 10) + (4 × 5) + (8 × 2) = 60 + 20 + 16 = ₹96.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Value of ten-rupee notes: 6 × ₹10 = ₹60.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Value of five-rupee coins: 4 × ₹5 = ₹20.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Value of two-rupee coins: 8 × ₹2 = ₹16.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Total monetary value = 60 + 20 + 16 = ₹96.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 8, 'q': 'Which collection has the highest value: 12 five-rupee coins, 28 two-rupee coins, or 7 ten-rupee notes?', 'options': ['12 five-rupee coins', '28 two-rupee coins', '7 ten-rupee notes', 'All are equal'], 'ans': 2, 'hint': 'Calculate each value: 12 × 5, 28 × 2, and 7 × 10.', 'explanation': '12 × 5 = ₹60; 28 × 2 = ₹56; 7 × 10 = ₹70. ₹70 is the highest.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Calculate value 1: 12 × ₹5 = ₹60.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Calculate value 2: 28 × ₹2 = ₹56.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Calculate value 3: 7 × ₹10 = ₹70.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Comparing: ₹70 > ₹60 > ₹56. 7 ten-rupee notes has the highest value.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 9, 'q': 'An orchard has 35 coconut trees arranged in 5 equal rows. How many trees are in each row?', 'options': ['6 trees', '7 trees', '8 trees', '9 trees'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'Divide the total count of 35 trees by 5 rows.', 'explanation': '35 ÷ 5 = 7 trees in each row.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Total trees = 35, organized in 5 equal rows.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Divide: 35 ÷ 5 = 7 trees per row.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 10, 'q': 'If 10 ten-rupee notes and 8 five-rupee coins are used to pay a bill of ₹135, how much change is returned?', 'options': ['₹0 (exact)', '₹5', '₹10', '₹15'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'Total paid = (10 × 10) + (8 × 5) = 100 + 40 = 140 rupees. Change = 140 - 135.', 'explanation': 'Total paid = ₹140. Change returned = 140 - 135 = ₹5.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Total money paid = (10 × ₹10) + (8 × ₹5) = ₹100 + ₹40 = ₹140.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Bill amount = ₹135.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Change returned = ₹140 - ₹135 = ₹5.</li></ol></div>"}]}, {'id': 18, 'title': 'Challenge 2', 'section': 'Mastery Challenges', 'speech': '', 'quiz': [{'id': 11, 'q': 'What is the minimum number of notes and coins needed to form ₹68 using ₹50, ₹10, ₹5, ₹2, and ₹1 denominations?', 'options': ['4 items', '5 items', '6 items', '7 items'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'Use the largest denominations first: 1 × ₹50, 1 × ₹10, 1 × ₹5, 1 × ₹2, 1 × ₹1.', 'explanation': '1 × ₹50 + 1 × ₹10 + 1 × ₹5 + 1 × ₹2 + 1 × ₹1 = ₹68. Total = 5 items.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Start with largest denomination: 1 × ₹50 = ₹50 (remaining ₹18).</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Next denomination: 1 × ₹10 = ₹10 (remaining ₹8).</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Next denomination: 1 × ₹5 = ₹5 (remaining ₹3).</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Next denomination: 1 × ₹2 = ₹2 (remaining ₹1).</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Final denomination: 1 × ₹1 = ₹1 (remaining ₹0).</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Total count of items = 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 5 items.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 12, 'q': 'Can ₹125 be formed using exactly 1 hundred-rupee note, 1 twenty-rupee note, and 1 five-rupee coin?', 'options': ['No, it falls short by ₹5', 'No, it exceeds by ₹5', 'Yes, exactly 100 + 20 + 5 = ₹125', 'Only if ₹2 coins are added'], 'ans': 2, 'hint': 'Add the denominations: 100 + 20 + 5.', 'explanation': '100 + 20 + 5 = ₹125 exactly.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>1 hundred-rupee note = ₹100.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>1 twenty-rupee note = ₹20.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>1 five-rupee coin = ₹5.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Sum: 100 + 20 + 5 = ₹125. Exactly matching.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 13, 'q': 'Which of the following numbers of coins CAN be grouped into 2 equal rows without any leftover?', 'options': ['15 coins', '18 coins', '21 coins', '25 coins'], 'ans': 1, 'exp': '18 ÷ 2 = 9 coins per row with zero remainder. 18 is an even number.', 'hint': 'Check which number is an even number divisible by 2.', 'explanation': '18 is even (18 = 2 × 9), so it splits into 2 equal rows of 9 coins.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>To divide into 2 equal rows with no leftovers, the quantity must be even.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Test each: 15 (odd, leaves 1), 18 (even, 18 ÷ 2 = 9), 21 (odd), 25 (odd).</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>18 coins forms 2 equal rows of 9 coins.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 14, 'q': 'If 24 coins are arranged into 4 equal columns, how many coins are in each column?', 'options': ['5 coins', '6 coins', '7 coins', '8 coins'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'Divide 24 coins by 4 columns.', 'explanation': '24 ÷ 4 = 6 coins per column.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Total coins = 24. Number of columns = 4.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Divide: 24 ÷ 4 = 6 coins in each column.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 15, 'q': 'How many individual shoes are there in 9 complete pairs of shoes?', 'options': ['16 shoes', '18 shoes', '20 shoes', '22 shoes'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'Each pair consists of 2 shoes.', 'explanation': '9 pairs × 2 shoes per pair = 18 shoes.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>1 pair = 2 items.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>9 pairs = 9 × 2 = 18 shoes.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 16, 'q': 'When 19 pencils are grouped into pairs of two, how many pencils remain unpaired?', 'options': ['0 pencils', '1 pencil', '2 pencils', '3 pencils'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': '19 is an odd number. Dividing by 2 always leaves a remainder.', 'explanation': '19 = (9 × 2) + 1. There are 9 pairs and exactly 1 unpaired pencil.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Divide 19 by 2: 19 ÷ 2 = 9 complete pairs with a remainder of 1.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Exactly 1 pencil remains unpaired.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 17, 'q': 'Which of these numbers is an even number: 13, 17, 20, 23?', 'options': ['13', '17', '20', '23'], 'ans': 2, 'hint': 'Even numbers end in 0, 2, 4, 6, or 8 and divide by 2 with no remainder.', 'explanation': '20 ends in 0 and divides evenly by 2 (20 ÷ 2 = 10).', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Check divisibility by 2 or ending digit.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>13, 17, and 23 end in 3, 7, and 3 (all odd).</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>20 ends in 0, so 20 is an even number.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 18, 'q': 'If an even number is divided into two equal halves, what kind of numbers are the two halves?', 'options': ['Always fractions', 'Always equal whole numbers', 'Always odd numbers', 'Always unequal numbers'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'Think of 8 ÷ 2 = 4 and 4, or 10 ÷ 2 = 5 and 5.', 'explanation': 'An even number divides into two identical whole numbers.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>By definition, an even number 2n divides by 2 to yield two identical whole numbers n and n.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 19, 'q': 'How can ₹183 be assembled with the fewest notes and coins using standard denominations?', 'options': ['18 ten-rupee notes and 3 one-rupee coins', '1 hundred, 1 fifty, 3 tens, 1 two-rupee, and 1 one-rupee', '3 fifty-rupee notes and 33 one-rupee coins', '2 fifty-rupee notes and 83 one-rupee coins'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'Combine ₹100 + ₹50 + ₹30 + ₹2 + ₹1.', 'explanation': '1 × 100 + 1 × 50 + 3 × 10 + 1 × 2 + 1 × 1 = ₹183 (7 items total).', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>100 + 50 = 150.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>150 + 30 (three ₹10 notes) = 180.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>180 + 2 + 1 = 183.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>This uses only 7 items and is the standard minimal denomination set.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 20, 'q': 'Which statement about zero is mathematically correct?', 'options': ['Zero is an odd number', 'Zero is an even number', 'Zero is neither even nor odd', 'Zero cannot be tested'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'Zero divided by 2 gives 0 with remainder 0.', 'explanation': '0 ÷ 2 = 0 remainder 0. Since the remainder is 0, zero is an even number.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Dividing 0 by 2 yields a quotient of 0 with 0 remainder.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Because there is zero remainder, 0 satisfies the definition of an even number.</li></ol></div>"}]}, {'id': 19, 'title': 'Challenge 3', 'section': 'Mastery Challenges', 'speech': '', 'quiz': [{'id': 21, 'q': 'What is the remainder when the odd number 37 is divided by 2?', 'options': ['0', '1', '2', '7'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'All odd numbers leave the same remainder when divided by 2.', 'explanation': '37 = (18 × 2) + 1. The remainder is always 1.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>37 ÷ 2 = 18 with 1 remainder.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Every odd number leaves a remainder of 1 when divided by 2.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 22, 'q': 'Which of the following is NOT an odd number: 11, 15, 18, 23?', 'options': ['11', '15', '18', '23'], 'ans': 2, 'hint': 'Look for the number ending in an even digit (0, 2, 4, 6, 8).', 'explanation': '18 ends in 8, which is an even digit, making 18 an even number.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>11 ends in 1 (odd).</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>15 ends in 5 (odd).</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>18 ends in 8 (even).</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>23 ends in 3 (odd).</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>18 is the only even number in the list.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 23, 'q': 'How many odd numbers are there in the sequence from 1 to 10 inclusive?', 'options': ['4', '5', '6', '7'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'List them: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9.', 'explanation': 'There are 5 odd numbers (1, 3, 5, 7, 9) and 5 even numbers (2, 4, 6, 8, 10).', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Odd numbers in 1 to 10: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Count = 5 odd numbers.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 24, 'q': 'If you multiply any whole number by 2, what will always be the parity of the product?', 'options': ['Always odd', 'Always even', 'Sometimes odd, sometimes even', 'Depends on the number'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'Any number multiplied by 2 consists of complete pairs of 2.', 'explanation': 'Multiplying by 2 ensures complete pairing with zero remainder, so the product is always even.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Let n be any integer. 2 × n = 2n, which is divisible by 2 with 0 remainder.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Therefore, multiplying any whole number by 2 always yields an even number.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 25, 'q': 'In a 1–50 crayon chart, in which column would you find the number 29?', 'options': ['Column ending in 2', 'Column ending in 5', 'Column ending in 7', 'Column ending in 9'], 'ans': 3, 'hint': 'The ones digit indicates the column in a 10-column grid.', 'explanation': '29 ends in 9, so it is in the 9th column (the column of numbers ending in 9).', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>In a standard 10-column grid, column index matches the ones digit.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>29 ends in 9, placing it in column 9 (which contains only circled odd numbers).</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 26, 'q': 'What marker would be placed on number 44 in the crayon grid: circle or square?', 'options': ['Circle because 44 is odd', 'Square box because 44 is even', 'Both circle and square', 'Neither'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': '44 ends in 4, so test if it is even or odd.', 'explanation': '44 ends in 4 (an even digit), so 44 is even and receives a square box marker.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Check the ones digit of 44: it is 4.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Since 4 is even, 44 is an even number.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>According to the rule, even numbers are marked with square boxes.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 27, 'q': 'If you open a textbook to a spread and the left page is page 56, what is the right page number?', 'options': ['Page 55', 'Page 57', 'Page 58', 'Page 60'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'The right page directly follows the left page in reading sequence.', 'explanation': '56 + 1 = 57. The right page is page 57.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Left page = 56 (an even page).</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Right page = Left page + 1 = 56 + 1 = 57 (an odd page).</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 28, 'q': 'What is the sum of facing pages 28 and 29, and what is its parity?', 'options': ['56, which is even', '57, which is odd', '58, which is even', '59, which is odd'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'Add 28 + 29, then check the ones digit of the sum.', 'explanation': '28 + 29 = 57. Since 57 ends in 7, the sum is odd.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Calculate: 28 + 29 = 57.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Check parity: 57 ends in 7, which is an odd digit.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>An even number + an odd number always produces an odd sum: 57 is odd.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 29, 'q': 'Why is the left page of a printed book always an even number in standard publishing?', 'options': ['Because books always start with page 0', 'Because page 1 starts on the right side of the first leaf, making its reverse page 2 on the left', 'Publishers choose page numbers at random', 'Because odd numbers cannot be printed on the left'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'Consider the very first page (page 1) on the right side of the first sheet.', 'explanation': 'Page 1 is on the recto (right). Its reverse side is page 2 (left), so all left pages are even.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Leaf 1 front = Page 1 (Right side).</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Leaf 1 back = Page 2 (Left side).</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Each subsequent sheet advances by 2 pages, keeping left pages even (2, 4, 6...) and right pages odd (1, 3, 5...).</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 30, 'q': 'Which pair of numbers represents two facing pages in an open book?', 'options': ['Page 31 and Page 32', 'Page 34 and Page 35', 'Page 40 and Page 42', 'Page 25 and Page 27'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'Facing pages are consecutive: left page must be EVEN, right page must be ODD.', 'explanation': 'Page 34 is even (left) and Page 35 is odd (right). Page 31 on left is impossible.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>A facing spread requires: Left page = Even, Right page = Even + 1 (Odd).</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>In Option 1: 31 is odd, so it cannot be on the left of a spread.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>In Option 2: 34 is even (left) and 35 is odd (right), which matches standard book format.</li></ol></div>"}]}, {'id': 20, 'title': 'Challenge 4', 'section': 'Mastery Challenges', 'speech': '', 'quiz': [{'id': 31, 'q': 'What is the single most important rule to determine if a multi-digit number like 4,785 is even or odd?', 'options': ['Add all the digits together', 'Look only at the first digit on the left', 'Look only at the ones digit on the far right', 'Divide the whole number by 10'], 'ans': 2, 'hint': 'All tens, hundreds, and thousands are multiples of 10, which are already even.', 'explanation': 'Only the ones digit determines parity. Since 4,785 ends in 5, it is odd.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Multiples of 10, 100, and 1,000 are always even.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Parity is determined solely by the ones digit.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>4,785 ends in 5, so it is odd.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 32, 'q': 'Classify these four numbers: 30, 46, 78, 67. How many are even?', 'options': ['1 is even', '2 are even', '3 are even', 'All 4 are even'], 'ans': 2, 'hint': 'Check the ending digit of each: 0, 6, 8 are even; 7 is odd.', 'explanation': '30, 46, and 78 end in 0, 6, and 8 (even). 67 ends in 7 (odd). Thus, 3 are even.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>30 ends in 0 -> Even.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>46 ends in 6 -> Even.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>78 ends in 8 -> Even.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>67 ends in 7 -> Odd.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Total even numbers = 3.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 33, 'q': 'Using the digits 3 and 8 without repetition, which 2-digit numbers can you form, and what is their parity?', 'options': ['38 is odd, 83 is even', '38 is even, 83 is odd', 'Both 38 and 83 are even', 'Both 38 and 83 are odd'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': '38 ends in 8; 83 ends in 3.', 'explanation': '38 ends in 8 (even), so 38 is even. 83 ends in 3 (odd), so 83 is odd.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Formed numbers: 38 and 83.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>38 has ones digit 8 -> Even.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>83 has ones digit 3 -> Odd.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 34, 'q': 'If you are given two even digits (such as 4 and 6), can you form any odd 2-digit number using both?', 'options': ['Yes, by placing 4 first', 'Yes, by placing 6 first', 'No, impossible because both digits are even', 'Only if you add 1'], 'ans': 2, 'hint': 'Whichever digit is in the ones place will be an even digit.', 'explanation': 'The formed numbers 46 and 64 both end in even digits, so both are always even.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Possible numbers: 46 and 64.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Both ones digits (6 and 4) are even.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Therefore, it is impossible to form an odd number using only even digits.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 35, 'q': 'What are the immediate predecessor (number before) and successor (number after) of the odd number 75?', 'options': ['73 and 77 (both odd)', '74 and 76 (both even)', '74 and 77 (one even, one odd)', '75 has no neighbors'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': '75 - 1 = 74, and 75 + 1 = 76.', 'explanation': 'The predecessor is 74 and successor is 76. Both end in even digits (4 and 6), so both are even.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Number before 75 = 75 - 1 = 74 (Even).</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Number after 75 = 75 + 1 = 76 (Even).</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Any odd number is always sandwiched between two even neighbors.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 36, 'q': 'In a sequence of 6 consecutive whole numbers (for example 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16), how many are even and how many are odd?', 'options': ['2 even, 4 odd', '3 even, 3 odd', '4 even, 2 odd', 'Depends on the starting number'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'Consecutive numbers alternate strictly: odd, even, odd, even, odd, even.', 'explanation': 'In any 6 consecutive numbers, there are exactly 3 pairs of (odd, even), yielding 3 odd and 3 even numbers.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Consecutive numbers alternate: O, E, O, E, O, E.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Count of Odd = 3.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Count of Even = 3.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>They are perfectly balanced.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 37, 'q': 'How many even numbers are there between 1 and 100 inclusive?', 'options': ['40', '45', '50', '55'], 'ans': 2, 'hint': 'Exactly half of the 100 numbers are even.', 'explanation': '100 ÷ 2 = 50 even numbers (and 50 odd numbers).', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>The numbers 1 to 100 form 50 pairs of consecutive numbers (1,2), (3,4)... (99,100).</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Each pair has 1 even number.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Total even numbers = 50 × 1 = 50.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 38, 'q': 'What is the parity of the sum of two even numbers (such as 14 + 22 = 36)?', 'options': ['Always odd', 'Always even', 'Sometimes odd, sometimes even', 'Always ends in 0'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'Even numbers have zero remainder. Adding two zero remainders leaves zero remainder.', 'explanation': 'Even + Even = Even. 14 + 22 = 36, which is even.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>First even number: complete pairs with 0 remainder.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Second even number: complete pairs with 0 remainder.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Combined sum: all items pair up with 0 remainder -> Always Even.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 39, 'q': 'What is the parity of the sum of two odd numbers (such as 9 + 13 = 22)?', 'options': ['Always odd', 'Always even', 'Sometimes odd, sometimes even', 'Cannot be determined'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'Each odd number has 1 leftover item. When added, the two leftovers pair up: 1 + 1 = 2.', 'explanation': 'Odd + Odd = Even. The two remainders of 1 combine to form a complete pair.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>First odd number leaves 1 remainder.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Second odd number leaves 1 remainder.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Combine remainders: 1 + 1 = 2, which forms a complete new pair.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Therefore, Odd + Odd is ALWAYS Even.</li></ol></div>"}, {'id': 40, 'q': 'What is the parity of the sum of an even number and an odd number (such as 16 + 7 = 23)?', 'options': ['Always even', 'Always odd', 'Always zero', 'Depends on which number is larger'], 'ans': 1, 'hint': 'The even number has 0 remainder, while the odd number has 1 remainder: 0 + 1 = 1.', 'explanation': 'Even + Odd = Odd. 16 + 7 = 23, which is odd.', 'sol': "<div class='p-3 bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 rounded-lg text-sm'><div class='font-bold text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mb-2 flex items-center gap-1.5'><svg class='w-4 h-4' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' viewBox='0 0 24 24'><path stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' stroke-width='2' d='M13 16h-1v-4h-1m1-4h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z'></path></svg>Step-by-Step Solution</div><ol class='list-decimal list-inside space-y-1'><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Even number leaves 0 remainder.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Odd number leaves 1 remainder.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Total remainder = 0 + 1 = 1.</li><li class='mb-2 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300'>Since 1 leftover remains, Even + Odd is ALWAYS Odd.</li></ol></div>"}]}]
